+/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+   FRAME C · PASTEL — the tile skin (7.21 handoff, source-home/pastel-frame.css)
+   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+   WeekC IS the Frame C Week view, so it renders under Pastel. Its tile is a
+   DEEP subject gradient (58%/40% toward black) carrying WHITE text — the right
+   answer for the old "Color-forward" frame, and the wrong one for Pastel, whose
+   whole premise is soft tints on a light canvas. Left alone it produced the
+   live-QA symptom: a pale Pastel page with a row of near-black lesson cards.
+
+   THE FLIP IS ATOMIC — SURFACE AND INK MOVE TOGETHER. That is CLAUDE.md §4's
+   legibility contract, and it is the only real hazard in this block: lighten the
+   gradient without also darkening the ink and every tile becomes white-on-white.
+   `.tileTitle` / `.tileBodyRow` / `.tileBodyEmpty` all use `color: inherit`, so
+   they follow `.tile`'s colour for free; the two that hard-code white
+   (`.tileUnit`, and the focus ring) are re-pointed explicitly below.
